{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"e85fe2a5112eb320","name":"card_unit_tiger_moth","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Britain/WorldAtWar/units/card_unit_tiger_moth","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"TIGER MOTH","text":"Deployment: Give a friendly unit +1 defense, then change its attack to equal its defense.","flavorText":"The de Havilland Tiger Moth was Britain’s standard trainer aircraft throughout WWII. First flown in 1931, it entered service in the RAF in 1932 and quickly became the cornerstone of Allied pilot training. Over 8,000 were produced in Britain, Canada, Australia and other countries, and thousands of pilots from across the Commonwealth earned their wings in this plane. Although it was designed for basic flight instruction, the Tiger Moth took on unexpected wartime roles. In 1939, RAF Coastal Command organized “scarecrow patrols” using unarmed Tiger Moths to deter German U-boats and force them to dive. Lacking radios, crews carried homing pigeons for emergencies. A few were even armed with small bombs, though none were confirmed used in combat. After the 1940 campaign in France, Britain prepared for possible invasion. Around 350 Tiger Moths were fitted with bomb racks to serve as emergency light bombers under Operation Banquet. Some were tested with scythe-like devices intended to slash parachutes mid-air. Others were equipped to disperse toxic dust like Paris green against enemy troops. Despite its fragile appearance, the Tiger Moth proved surprisingly adaptable. It played no direct combat role, but it proved to be a vital link in the Allied air effort worldwide, from training fields to improvised defensive missions.","faction":"Britain","type":"bomber","rarity":"Rare","cost":3,"operationCost":1,"attack":1,"defense":3},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_unit_tiger_moth","title":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_tiger_moth_title","SourceString":"TIGER MOTH","LocalizedString":"TIGER MOTH"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::bomber","attack":1,"defense":3,"range":2,"faction":"EFactionEnum::Britain","kredits":3,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_tiger_moth_text","SourceString":"Deployment: Give a friendly unit +1 defense, then change its attack to equal its defense.","LocalizedString":"Deployment: Give a friendly unit +1 defense, then change its attack to equal its defense."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_tiger_moth_history","SourceString":"The de Havilland Tiger Moth was Britain’s standard trainer aircraft throughout WWII. First flown in 1931, it entered service in the RAF in 1932 and quickly became the cornerstone of Allied pilot training. Over 8,000 were produced in Britain, Canada, Australia and other countries, and thousands of pilots from across the Commonwealth earned their wings in this plane. Although it was designed for basic flight instruction, the Tiger Moth took on unexpected wartime roles. In 1939, RAF Coastal Command organized “scarecrow patrols” using unarmed Tiger Moths to deter German U-boats and force them to dive. Lacking radios, crews carried homing pigeons for emergencies. A few were even armed with small bombs, though none were confirmed used in combat. After the 1940 campaign in France, Britain prepared for possible invasion. Around 350 Tiger Moths were fitted with bomb racks to serve as emergency light bombers under Operation Banquet. Some were tested with scythe-like devices intended to slash parachutes mid-air. Others were equipped to disperse toxic dust like Paris green against enemy troops. Despite its fragile appearance, the Tiger Moth proved surprisingly adaptable. It played no direct combat role, but it proved to be a vital link in the Allied air effort worldwide, from training fields to improvised defensive missions.","LocalizedString":"The de Havilland Tiger Moth was Britain’s standard trainer aircraft throughout WWII. First flown in 1931, it entered service in the RAF in 1932 and quickly became the cornerstone of Allied pilot training. Over 8,000 were produced in Britain, Canada, Australia and other countries, and thousands of pilots from across the Commonwealth earned their wings in this plane. Although it was designed for basic flight instruction, the Tiger Moth took on unexpected wartime roles. In 1939, RAF Coastal Command organized “scarecrow patrols” using unarmed Tiger Moths to deter German U-boats and force them to dive. Lacking radios, crews carried homing pigeons for emergencies. A few were even armed with small bombs, though none were confirmed used in combat. After the 1940 campaign in France, Britain prepared for possible invasion. Around 350 Tiger Moths were fitted with bomb racks to serve as emergency light bombers under Operation Banquet. Some were tested with scythe-like devices intended to slash parachutes mid-air. Others were equipped to disperse toxic dust like Paris green against enemy troops. Despite its fragile appearance, the Tiger Moth proved surprisingly adaptable. It played no direct combat role, but it proved to be a vital link in the Allied air effort worldwide, from training fields to improvised defensive missions."},"operationCost":1,"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Rare","selectTargetOnPlayedFromHand":true,"cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::NavalWarfare","hasDeployment":true,"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundCue'AU_depl_biplane_Cue'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Assets/Audio/Biplane/AU_depl_biplane_Cue.0"},"soundMoveToFrontline":{"ObjectName":"SoundCue'AU_move_biplane_01_Cue'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Assets/Audio/Biplane/AU_move_biplane_01_Cue.0"},"soundAttack":{"ObjectName":"SoundCue'AU_move_biplane_01_Cue'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Assets/Audio/Biplane/AU_move_biplane_01_Cue.0"},"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/Britain/World_at_War/T_tiger_moth.T_tiger_moth","SubPathString":""},"artist":"Michael Turner","historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/TIGER_MOTH.TIGER_MOTH","SubPathString":""},"histo